  "question": {
    "text": "Let $x \\in(0,1)$. The set of all $x$ such that $\\sin ^{-1} x>\\cos ^{-1} x$, is the interval:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "uEyq9",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "01u9V",
      "text": "\n$\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}, 1\\right)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "4dZyX",
      "text": "\n$(0,1)$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "hv3gn",
      "text": "\n$\\left(0, \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given $\\sin ^{-1} x>\\cos ^{-1} x$ where $x \\in(0,1)$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Rightarrow \\sin ^{-1} x>\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\sin ^{-1} x \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow 2 \\sin ^{-1} x>\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\Rightarrow \\sin ^{-1} x>\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow x>\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\Rightarrow x>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMaximum value of $\\sin ^{-1} x$ is $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\nSo, maximum value of $x$ is 1 . So, $x \\in\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}, 1\\right)$",
    "image": null
  },
